tests/harness/mod.rs\n  138 translation/ERRORS.md\n  930 total\n\n########## final: cargo test ##########\ntest result: ok. 0 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.00s\ntest result: ok. 29 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.09s","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_z364so7beecugs4j5yuilc7fqhynwa7tjofyxcgiwjlbb62vt7cq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four phases complete. Summary:\n\n## Phase A — both build\n- **C**: `cmake -S c_src -B build && cmake --build build` → `c_src/build/driver`\n- **Rust**: `cd translation && cargo build --release` → `translation/target/release/driver` (compiled with no errors as-is)\n\n## The C program's actual shape\n`main()` → `helloworld()` → one `printf(\"Hello World!\\n\")` → `return 0`. It is **branch-free**: no `scanf`/`fgets`, no `argc`/`argv` use, no length or null checks, no early returns, no error path. So the input classes aren't \"values it parses\" — they're the ways the process can be invoked and the ways its single write can fail. That's where the bug was.\n\n## Phase B/C — 1 real mismatch found and fixed\n\n**Exit status on SIGPIPE.** When stdout's reader has hung up:\n\n| | C | Rust (before) |\n|---|---|---|\n| stdout | empty | empty |\n| exit status | **killed by signal 13** | **exited 0** |\n\nCause: Rust's runtime sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main`; a C program from a shell inherits the default. The C dies from the signal; the Rust just got `EPIPE`, ignored it, and exited 0. **Notably, a stdout-only test can never catch this** — both stdouts are byte-identical (empty), and only the status differs. Fixed in `src/main.rs` by resetting `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_DFL` via a dependency-free `extern \"C\"` `signal(2)`.\n\nI also confirmed the existing `let _ = write_all(...)` (rather than `println!`) is *required*, not incidental: under `> /dev/full` or `>&-` the C silently exits 0, while a `println!` translation panics with exit 101 and a stderr message.\n\n## Phase D — gate\n- 29 tests, **0 ignored/skipped/disabled**, passing in both debug and release\n- Covered: stdin shapes (absent/empty/no-trailing-newline/1000 lines/NUL & non-UTF-8/100k-char line/1 MiB via file *and* pipe/fd 0 closed), argv shapes (none/64/empty string/flag-lookalikes/100k-char/non-UTF-8), locales incl. `tr_TR.UTF-8`, output channels (pipe/file/closed fd/`/dev/full`/real pty), cwd, and 20-run determinism\n- **Mutation-tested** the suite to prove it can fail: dropping the `\\n`, returning 1, swapping in `println!`, and removing the SIGPIPE fix were each caught, then reverted\n- `c_src/` sources untouched (original mtimes/sizes verified); the harness builds C **out-of-tree** into the Cargo target dir when no prebuilt binary exists, so `cargo test` is self-contained without writing into `c_src/`\n\nTwo notes on things I deliberately hardened rather than left as-is: the SIGPIPE test creates its pipe by hand and closes the read end *before* spawning (dropping it after spawn races the child's write and would be flaky in both directions), and it asserts positively that the C side really was killed by signal 13 so it can't silently degrade into \"both exited 0\". The `/dev/full` and `script(1)` tests fall back to asserting the ordinary differential case rather than passing vacuously if those facilities are missing.\n\nMismatch and rationale are recorded in `translation/ERRORS.md`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1337,"cache_read_input_tokens":63309,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1337,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2a561947-18b4-41e6-828f-ea0d9a30292c","uuid":"ef19c90a-3875-4612-976a-56c54f8e5f5f","timestamp":"2026-08-28T07:56:42.025Z"}
